==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|biker}}, from {{der|en|non|bikarr|t=cup}}, from {{der|en|osx|bikeri|t=cup}}, from {{der|en|LL.|bīcārium|t=wine vat, jug}}, of disputed origin. Possibly from {{der|en|grc|βῖκος|t=earthenware jug, wine jar}}, or from {{der|en|la|bacarium|t=wine vat, vase}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Bieker|t=mug, cup, beaker}}, {{cog|nl|beker|t=beaker, cup}}, {{cog|de|Becher|t=beaker, cup, goblet}}, {{cog|da|bæger|t=beaker}}, {{cog|it|bicchiere|t=cup, glass (for drink)}}. See also {{l|en|pitcher}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbiːkə(ɹ)/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˈbiːkɚ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-beaker.ogg|Audio (AU)}}
* {{rhymes|en|iːkə(r)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A flat-bottomed [[vessel]], with a [[lip]], used as a [[laboratory]] [[container]].
# A [[drinking]] vessel without a [[handle]], sometimes for the use of [[children]].
# A [[mug]].
# {{lb|en|slang|Antarctica}} A [[scientist]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2008|author=Kim Stanley Robinson|title=Antarctica|page=52|passage={{...}} at every meal break he shambled into the galley black-fingered and smelling of engine-grease and concrete floors, to contemplate over his meal the '''beakers''' at their round tables chatting away, completely oblivious {{...}}}}

